package com.veilbreaker.companion

/** Offline field interpretation. These examples are not device measurements or universal pass criteria. */
internal object WifiReferenceDetails {
    private const val ANDROID = "Android WifiInfo reference (reviewed 2026-11-12): https://developer.android.com/reference/android/net/wifi/WifiInfo"
    private const val SCAN = "Android ScanResult and scan behavior (reviewed 2026-11-12): https://developer.android.com/reference/android/net/wifi/ScanResult · https://developer.android.com/develop/connectivity/wifi/wifi-scan"
    private const val CISCO = "Cisco Wireless RF Reference Guide (reviewed 2026-11-12): https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/wireless/controller/9800/technical-reference/wireless-rf-reference-guide.html"
    private const val PSC = "Cisco Wi-Fi 6E Band Operations and Client Connectivity (reviewed 2026-11-12): https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/wireless/catalyst-9166-series-access-points/220526-configure-and-verify-wi-fi-6e-band-opera.html"
    private const val FCC = "U.S. rules, 47 CFR §15.407 (reviewed 2026-11-12): https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-47/part-15/section-15.407"

    fun sections(id: String): List<Pair<String, String>> = when (id) {
        "wifi-rssi" -> listOf(
            "Interpret the number" to "RSSI is received power at the reporting radio, not the AP's transmit power. For a single comparable receiver, −55 dBm is 10 dB stronger than −65 dBm: that is ten times the received power. It does not imply ten times the throughput. Signal at the AP from a client can differ from the client's reading of the AP.\n\nAndroid may return the highest associated-link RSSI on a multi-link connection; it is not necessarily every radio's value. $ANDROID",
            "Field procedure" to "At each point, retain time, device, BSSID if exposed, band and channel. Sample in the user's normal orientation; repeat at the same height and locations. Compare receive power with actual service and quality measurements. A strong reading with poor performance warrants checks for contention, retransmissions and backhaul; it does not identify the cause. A numeric target belongs to a specific client and design, not every Wi-Fi link. $CISCO")
        "wifi-rate" -> listOf(
            "Read the reported rate" to "Link rate is a current PHY-layer negotiated value, not a transfer measurement or guaranteed throughput. Android may report a link speed for the associated MLO link with the highest RSSI; transmit and receive rates can differ. A missing value is unknown, not zero. $ANDROID",
            "Compare with service" to "Use a known test endpoint, interface, direction and duration; repeat under comparable load. For example, a 600 Mbps reported link rate and 250 Mbps measured application download differ by 350 Mbps, but that gap is not a direct overhead or fault measurement. Airtime sharing, retries, protocol overhead and remote-path limits can all contribute. Check both ends before changing RF settings. $CISCO")
        "wifi-identity" -> listOf(
            "Identify the connection" to "SSID is the network name; multiple BSSIDs can advertise it. BSSID is a basic service set identifier, not a reliable physical AP serial number. Android may redact SSID or BSSID; it may report the association link BSSID for multi-link operation. Treat absent identity as unavailable. $ANDROID",
            "Diagnose changes" to "During a repeatable walk, log time, BSSID, band, signal and any actual application interruption. A BSSID change can accompany roaming, but does not alone measure handoff time or prove failure. Compare with AP/controller events and the same client before attributing a symptom to a roam. $CISCO")
        "wifi-channels" -> listOf(
            "Frequency and width" to "A primary channel indicates one part of a connection; channel width and center frequencies describe the larger occupied allocation. Two networks can share a channel or overlap when wider blocks intersect. At 2.4 GHz, channel centers are 5 MHz apart, while a typical channel is 20 MHz wide; nearby channel numbers therefore are not necessarily independent. $CISCO",
            "Compare like observations" to "Record band, primary channel, width, center frequency, observation age and whether the device is connected or scanning. A list of nearby BSSIDs is not a measurement of channel busy time; cached results and hidden or unobserved radios can make it incomplete. Check current authorization and the equipment's regulatory domain before changing channels. $SCAN")
        "expanded-wifi-24" -> listOf(
            "U.S. 20 MHz reference" to "Common non-overlapping choices are channel 1 = 2412 MHz, channel 6 = 2437 MHz, and channel 11 = 2462 MHz. The 25 MHz center separation reduces adjacent channel overlap for the usual 20 MHz plan. Non-overlapping channels can still have co-channel contention from other devices on the same channel. $CISCO",
            "Field workflow" to "Record each AP's configured channel and width, then inspect neighboring observations at representative points. Avoid interpreting a channel list as measured airtime. Recheck after changes and during normal occupancy; Bluetooth, microwave ovens and other non-Wi-Fi devices can affect this band without appearing as access points. $SCAN")
        "expanded-wifi-5" -> listOf(
            "Channel and DFS context" to "5 GHz contains multiple channel groups, not one contiguous set available to every device. In applicable channels, dynamic frequency selection protects incumbent radar users; equipment may delay channel use or move after detecting radar. Exact allowed channels, power, indoor/outdoor conditions and detection behavior depend on current rules, hardware and configuration. $FCC",
            "Investigation" to "Record the regulatory domain, primary channel, width and the AP's event log around a reported outage. Check whether the client supports the selected channel and whether an observed move coincides with a DFS event. Do not infer a radar detection merely because a channel changed. Verify the current rules and manufacturer documentation before applying a plan. $CISCO")
        "expanded-wifi-6" -> listOf(
            "Preferred scanning channels" to "Common 6 GHz preferred scanning channel numbers are 5, 21, 37, 53, 69, 85, 101, 117, 133, 149, 165, 181, 197, 213 and 229. They are 80 MHz apart and aid discovery; a PSC is a discovery aid, not proof that all operation must be on that channel or that a channel is authorized everywhere. $PSC",
            "Check a deployment" to "Confirm client 6 GHz capability, the AP's operating mode and current local authorization, then observe actual discovery and association. Absence from a phone's cached list is not proof that an AP is not transmitting. Check security requirements and client/AP settings for the actual deployment. $SCAN")
        "expanded-wifi-width" -> listOf(
            "Bandwidth tradeoff" to "20, 40, 80, 160 and 320 MHz describe channel width. Doubling width can increase the PHY resources available under compatible conditions, but does not guarantee doubled application throughput. Wider channels consume more spectrum and may intersect more neighboring allocations. Capability and permitted channel combinations vary by band and equipment. $CISCO",
            "Practical comparison" to "Record primary and center channels, width, client, target, time and load. Compare candidate configurations using repeat tests in both directions, latency, retries and AP-side busy-time metrics where available. A lower measured rate with a wider setting does not, by itself, prove that width caused the difference. $ANDROID")
        "expanded-wifi-snr" -> listOf(
            "Calculation and example" to "When signal and noise are measured at the same receiver with compatible bandwidth, SNR in dB = signal in dBm − noise in dBm. Example: −65 dBm signal and −90 dBm noise yield 25 dB SNR. The example is arithmetic, not a measured reading or a universal acceptance threshold. $CISCO",
            "Do not invent the noise floor" to "A phone may report RSSI without a compatible noise measurement. When noise is not exposed, show SNR as unavailable rather than assuming −90 dBm. Record measurement bandwidth, receiver, time and method; interference may vary in time and is not identified by an SNR value alone. $ANDROID")
        "expanded-wifi-roam" -> listOf(
            "What can be observed" to "A change in BSSID can show that the phone reports a different associated basic service set. It does not by itself establish why the client moved, how long a handoff took, or whether an application was interrupted. MLO and identity redaction can complicate the view. $ANDROID",
            "Repeatable test" to "Start a continuous application or suitable measurement on a known path, then walk the same route at a similar pace. Record timestamps, client, BSSID, channel, signal and any observed interruption. Correlate with AP/controller logs where available; repeat after any change before treating it as a fix. $CISCO")
        "expanded-wifi-mcs" -> listOf(
            "Why PHY rates vary" to "The PHY rate depends on the negotiated technology and operating parameters such as modulation/coding, channel width, spatial streams and guard interval. Rate adaptation can change these with conditions. A phone's link speed is not necessarily enough to reconstruct every MCS parameter, and it is not throughput. $ANDROID",
            "Field interpretation" to "Record both directional rates when exposed, channel width, band and client type, then compare a timed transfer to a known endpoint. Low application throughput with a high reported PHY rate may have several causes; check retries, airtime and the path beyond Wi-Fi rather than inferring a specific MCS or fault. $CISCO")
        "expanded-wifi-airtime" -> listOf(
            "Airtime and load" to "Stations on a channel share access to the medium. Slow transmissions and retransmissions can occupy more airtime for the same payload; management traffic and neighboring networks also consume time. Signal strength and number of visible BSSIDs are not direct channel-busy measurements. $CISCO",
            "Collect useful evidence" to "Measure over representative busy and quiet intervals. Compare AP/controller utilization and retry counters, client mix, application delay and an appropriate throughput test on the same band and channel. Record counter definitions and interval; a single scan or an isolated busy percentage does not prove congestion. $SCAN")
        else -> emptyList()
    }
}
